  "title": "Did the live horizon rows start moving toward the naive reference?",
  "why_this_exists": "Two independent reads of the same live official pages (this session and PR #86, both at 18:40Z-19:10Z) found that 18 of 30 team records were one game light in the published capture (the 11/13 finals) and that 19 of 30 team advanced-rating rows had moved. The natural question is whether the live ratings have drifted away from the naive reference that the model is supposed to beat. This file measures that directly, and separately states why it cannot be read as a performance result.",
  "method": "model_spread_home minus ref_spread_home on every horizon row (live ratings) and on every out-of-sample row (frozen ratings), compared on the same statistic. No model was re-fitted and no line was re-cut.",
  "what_this_proves": "Whether the live line set and the reference line disagree by more or less than they did on the out-of-sample window.",
  "what_this_does_NOT_prove": "Anything about skill. The live horizon rows carry ratings that may have seen results the out-of-sample rows never saw, and none of the live games has been played yet, so there is no outcome to grade against. A larger gap on unplayed games is not an edge and is not published as one."
